.TH LS 1
.SH NAME
ls, l, lc, lf, lr, lx \- list the contents of directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ls
[\ \-1ACFRabcdfghilmnopqrstux\ ] [\ name\ ...\ ]
.br
.B l
[\ options\ ] [\ name\ ...\ ]
.br
.B lc
[\ options\ ] [\ name\ ...\ ]
.br
.B lf
[\ options\ ] [\ name\ ...\ ]
.br
.B lr
[\ options\ ] [\ name\ ...\ ]
.br
.B lx
[\ options\ ] [\ name\ ...\ ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
For each directory named,
.B ls
lists its contents; for each other file, the file itself. With no names
it lists the current directory. Files named on the command line come
first, together, then each directory, under its name when there is more
than one. The names are sorted, one to a line unless an option asks for
another form.
.PP
The other names are
.B ls
with options already given:
.B l
is ls \-l,
.B lc
ls \-C,
.B lf
ls \-CF,
.B lr
ls \-CR and
.B lx
ls \-x.
.PP
The options are:
.TP 6
.B \-l
The long form: for each file its mode, links, owner, group, size in
bytes and time of last modification:
.RS
.PP
.nf
drwxr-xr-x   2 bin      bin           48 Jul  1  1986 lib
.fi
.PP
A time more than six months old, or in the future, shows the year in
place of the hour. A special file shows its major and minor device
numbers in place of a size. The first letter of the mode is
.B d
for a directory,
.B c
and
.B b
for character and block special files,
.B p
for a named pipe,
.B n
for a XENIX name space entry (a semaphore or shared data), and
.B \-
for an ordinary file. The permissions follow, for the owner, the group
and the others:
.B s
in place of the owner's or the group's
.B x
marks a set-user-id or set-group-id file
.RB ( S
when the file cannot be executed), and
.B t
in place of the others'
.B x
the sticky bit
.RB ( T
likewise).
.RE
.TP 6
.B \-n
As \-l, with the numbers of the owner and group for their names.
.TP
.B \-o
As \-l, without the group.
.TP
.B \-g
As \-l, without the owner.
.TP
.B \-h
With \-l and \-s, sizes in units of 1024: B for bytes, K for kilobytes,
M megabytes, G gigabytes and T terabytes, rounded, with a decimal place
below 10 (6.7K, 431K, 1.0M).
.TP
.B \-C
In columns, sorted down the columns.
.TP
.B \-x
In columns, sorted across the lines. With both \-C and \-x, the lines
win.
.TP
.B \-m
As a list across the page, separated by commas.
.TP
.B \-1
One to a line.
.TP
.B \-t
Sorted by the time of last modification, the newest first.
.TP
.B \-u
With \-t and \-l, the time of last access instead.
.TP
.B \-c
With \-t and \-l, the time the i-node last changed instead.
.TP
.B \-r
In the reverse order.
.TP
.B \-a
Every entry, those whose names begin with a dot too.
.TP
.B \-A
Every entry but . and ..; for the super-user, who sees them without it,
.B \-A
hides the names that begin with a dot.
.TP
.B \-d
A directory named is listed as a file, not opened.
.TP
.B \-R
The directories found in each directory are listed too, after it.
.TP
.B \-f
Each name is taken as a directory, and its entries are listed in the
order the directory holds them. \-f turns on \-a and turns off \-l, \-s,
\-t and \-r.
.TP
.B \-i
The i-number of each file, first.
.TP
.B \-s
The size of each file in blocks of 512 bytes, first, counting the
indirect blocks that find its data; a directory begins with the total.
.TP
.B \-F
A / after each directory and a * after each file that can be executed.
.TP
.B \-p
A / after each directory.
.TP
.B \-b
A character that cannot be printed shown as \e and its number in octal.
.TP
.B \-q
A character that cannot be printed shown as ?.
.PP
The forms are those of XENIX's own ls, line for line: in \-C, \-x and \-m
the total that \-s begins a directory with takes the place of the first
name, and \-m prints the numbers of \-i and \-s without padding.
.SH EXAMPLES
The files changed most recently first, with sizes easy to read:
.PP
.nf
	ls \-lht /usr/lib
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP 16
/etc/passwd
the names of the owners
.TP
/etc/group
the names of the groups
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
"\fIname\fP not found" for a name that does not exist; "\fIname\fP
unreadable" for a directory that cannot be read. The exit status is 2
after either, or after a mistake in the options, and 0 otherwise.
.SH SEE ALSO
chmod(1), find(1), df(1)
.SH HISTORY
An
.B ls
command appeared in Version 1 AT&T UNIX. The names
.BR l ,
.BR lc ,
.BR lf ,
.B lr
and
.B lx
are XENIX's. The
.B \-h
option is an addition of the xenix86-devsys system tools.
.SH AUTHORS
This ls was written for the xenix86-devsys system tools.
.SH BUGS
The columns are always 80 characters wide.
.PP
Files with the same time are listed under \-t in the reverse order of
their names; XENIX's ls lists them in the order its sort happens to
leave them, which is not always the same.
.PP
Names of owners and groups are cut to 8 characters.
